{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1482311-mummy-20th-anniversary-ideas","id":1482311,"title":"Mummy 20th Anniversary Ideas","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-05-29T18:14:45-07:00","id":1482311,"title":"Mummy 20th Anniversary Ideas","content":"A few thoughts on a hypothetical 20th anniversary release of Mummy.<br /> <br /> 1. In very ancient times the most learned mystics and occasionally ancient gods discovered the secret to true immortality, a means to imbue a petitioner with the power to walk between life, death and back again eternally. One such spell was used by Isis to give life to her son Horus. Another was crafted in the ancient Andean mountains (long before the Incan Empire, although they were the last major civilization to make wide use of the spell, and perhaps the empire to use it most extensively). A third was created by Chinese Daoist experimenting with the forces of Yin and Yang. Other versions likely exist, but ancient souls capable of making the journey are rare even by the standards of other supernaturals and making direct contact with other immortals is a slow process, although one the Egyptian reborn and their counterparts actively pursue.<br /> <br /> 2. Each of these original spells of life have been passed down, stolen, rewritten, and destroyed over the centuries. There are a few major variants among each spell. The most famous example is the book of Cabirus, used by Greek and Celtic successors to Egyptian knowledge to imbue immortality into their own cults. Broadly speaking each spell can be divided into the culture it originally came from, which regardless of whether it has been translated into a different form ultimately determines what otherwordly beings the immortality seeker will petition for new life. Each of these Resurrection cults is broadly on tentatively friendly terms with those who possess the same spell, although this is not certain and period of feuding and bloodshed have been known. For the purposes of this edition, I would count allied cults for Egyptians to include the Cabiri and Ishmaelite courts. They are on uneasy terms with beings like the Shemsu-heru, but they do serve the save gods to a certain extent. Making war upon each other ultimately only serves their enemies. The Andean spell of life, incidentally, has also migrated to other parts of central and south America. Despite the vast cultural differences between their societies the basics of their magic and nature remain the same, usually describing similar concepts under local terminology. <br /> <br /> 3. There is only one spell of life. But it has two applications. The first, used in ancient times, empowered a pious and learned being of a certain culture to journey into his native afterlife and face the judgments of his ancient gods. Few mortals were pious enough to attain Resurrection, but the millennia were long and numerous succeeding candidates were assembled. To the Egyptian mummies these successes are called the Imkhu. To the Andeans they have many names, the most familiar is the Incan term Malki. To the chinese daoists that were often simply called Sages. But as eons have passed and cultures have died and risen the required knowledge for so prestigious a persona slowly faded. More recent ages have known their own pious individuals for certain, but they had no easy means to enter the ancient underworlds of those earlier civilization nor possessed the cultural touchstones needed to please the old gods of the dead. Thus the second application of the spell. Less worthy souls were allowed for thousands of years to rest in the underworld. At times one of these souls, or a portion thereof, found a promising entity among the recently dead. By taking possession of the body and forming a willing partnership with this young soul the spirit could seek out immortality in ancient homelands. Every version of the spell of life can be used this way, and every ancient society that hosted Resurrection cults was occasionally visited by a walking corpse asking, demanding, or begging to be sent to the dark afterlives of the Egyptians, Andeans, Chinese and less well known lands of the dead. This happened in the earliest of days, but in the modern era is the more common of the two paths to immortality since the ancient knowledge has ebbed. One thing I would add in this edition is that every major cult has at least one version of the spell of life at at least one sacred locale. Some are lucky enough to have several sacred sites and magicians potent enough to work the magic of immortality upon a suitable corpse.<br /> <br /> 4. Circumstances by which a joining ancient soul can possess a younger body vary-<br /> a. On occasion, the underworld unleashes a vast and terrible ghost storm upon the lands of the dead. The after math after leaves devastation in its wake, but also causes the ancient gates to the afterlife to swing wide and the barriers between life and death to become paper thin. At such times large (comparatively speaking) numbers of ancient souls are known to seek suitable hosts for themselves. <br /> b. At other times, a powerful being of life and death will occasionally awaken and touch the land. This will often cause the surrounding flows of lifeforce (called Sekhem by the Egyptians) to experience a grand inundation like the flood waters of the Nile. Souls seeking resurrection can operate more freely within regions so blessed, and many will not waste the opportunity should they be lucky enough to be aware of it.<br /> c. On a far more direct and personal level, a single portion of a specific entity may obtain the freedom and strength to seek out a host if certain events take place. Often, this will include the modern discovery of said ancient's tomb, the use of some artifact sacred to the being, reverence or violation upon the corpse of the being, and so forth. In such times as the spirit feels offended by the activities of the mortals it may well take the opportunity to seek bloody revenge before seeking out a Resurrection cult. The death gods sense of morality is not always in line with our modern standards, and many of them see this as a righteous act for violating a sacred place. <br /> d. At times a foolish mortal unaffiliated with a Resurrection cult is lucky enough to find a working version of the spell of life and attempts to become an Imkhu after long study. 99% of the time this operation is doomed to fail because the modern ages lacks enough ancient knowledge for even the most dedicated modern scholar to properly navigate the afterlife. But sometimes the mystic gets far enough to be found by an ancient spirit, and sometimes such a spirit will take pity upon the foolish scholar. <br /> e. Other times there is some remnant of the ancient spirit's cult still active in the modern world, and it is by their actions that a vessel is found. Perhaps this involves a volunteer and decades of study, perhaps it involves and desperate soul begging the cult for eternity...or perhaps it involves a kidnapping and a ritual sacrifice. In any event, some spirits are convinced to partner with a vessel by their living cultists.<br /> f. And sometimes fate simply deems the time for an ancient to reincarnate (in a sense) to be right. These particular forms of resurrection often also involve a curious sort of deja vu- the people and events occurring to the modern soul often mirror the circumstances of the elder's life at a pivotal moment. This is particularly true if the ancient made some critical mistake or experienced a deep regret. Fate simply gives both being a second chance.<br /> <br /> 5. Whether Imkhu or Reborn, a mummy's power is in part defined by the culture from which his spell of life springs. For the Egyptian mummies this means their nature is defined along the lines of the different portions of their soul as seen in Egyptian mythology. Andean mummies are defined by the sacred directions and the mummies of the Dao are split into Yin, Yang, or a balance between the two. If a soul is deemed partly unworthy, it suffers a spiritual wound the Egyptians call a Judged One. These beings are not especially tied to any of their parent cults or cultures and can move freely among the other immortals, but are often despised or at best mistrusted by their peers. <br /> <br /> 6. Broadly each group has access to some form of magic based on their Dynasty (the splat for the immortal) that is somewhat universal among other reborn. E.G.- all mummies have at least a limited access to Alchemy, Necromancy, Amulets, Celestial magic, and Effigies. These are fairly universal forms of magic, so all reborn have some potential means of mastering one or more of the disciplines. Individual cults and cultures will have unique spells, but others are attainable for all reborn. Each spell of life also hosts a very specific form of magic native to that culture. Egyptian mummies know the art of Nomenclature and true names, Andean sorcerers know the magic of sacrifice, etc. <br /> <br /> 7. All reborn can experience a state called Semektet or the Withering. Each mummy has a pool of lifeforce to draw upon when working magic starting at ten plus two points per level of Balance (or similar cultural associations) the mummy has been awarded by his gods. If he loses all his Sekhem, he enters the state of Semektet. There is at first no outward changes to be noticed. Indeed, many mummies suffer early stages of Semektet without issue and continue to operate freely. But their passion for life visibly fades, and their aura grown pale and dull. To remove this state, a mummy must visit his associated homeland or return to the afterlife and be judged. Outside of Semektet a mummy is vibrant and alive, able to bear or sire children and intensely aware of his surroundings. Lifeforce can be recovered as normal even in the state of Semektet. Losing their entire reserve a second time causes the reborn to look pale and pallid. Subsequent losses progressively give the mummy a more withered, corpse like appearance. At stage 5 the reborn no longer has a choice- they must seek their ancient homeland in the living world or the solace of the afterlife<br /> <br /> 8. All mummies of all cultures have grim mockeries of themselves. For the Egyptians this is the Children of Anubis, hideous disease ridden creatures that serve the Egyptian conception of the Wyrm called Apophis. The Andean mummies likewise face servants of the Oblivion and the Malfeans that sleep beneath the Dark Kingdom of Obsidian, while the Daoists are eternally struggling against the servants of the Yama Kings. Would be reborn currently seeking resurrection on their journey to a cult that possesses the spell of life are vulnerable to capture and corruption by these various forces. The common paths across the globe that these animated dead walk on the way to Egypt, South America, or the far east are watched carefully by both sides in this war for immortal servants. <br /> <br /> 9. More of a side idea- the Children of Osiris are still vampiric. However, there is a certain ritual a being can invoke that effectively petitions the egyptian god to place his judgment upon the adherent. Most often this is a death sentence, often one fully desired by the blood drinking monster when the burdens of its cursed immortality become too hard to bear. Other times it is a miracle- a restoration to humanity and an end to an ancient curse. These blessed beings are usually the intermediaries and often the priests invoking the magic of resurrection upon the mummies found by the cult.<br /> <br /> 10. Mummies are too few and far between to have much in the way of a true society, even accounting for the Reborn. But the Resurrection cults are a hodge podge of scholars and soldiers and fanatics, each with their own long histories and goals. The mummies are uniquely positioned to be the mediators and uniters in these groups, as even the most jaded and neutral resurrection cult either worships, loves or fears the wrath of the gods the mummies routinely walk among and respect their word as that bridge between life and death. Horus created a Code by which his servants were to abide long ago. While not all reborn have sworn to follow said Code, it is the role (arrogant though it might be) of the Shemsu-heru to see that it is applied to the best of their ability across the world. Fortunately, beyond the Companions of Horus there was an older, less formal structure used by the Osirian League when various factions of Egypt united against Set and Apophis, which included allies as unlikely as vampires, werewolves, mages, wraiths and other beings. Horus has a system of engagement, negotiation and truce making in place that he has since applied to the world of immortals. It is largely under the auspices of this system that Mummies cautiously establish embassies in various cities, how cults build secret temples and lodges without stepping on each other's toes, how new tombs are constructed in modern cities and ancient ones are unearthed or rebuilt brick by brick in new regions. Officially no mummy outside the ranks of the Shemsu-heru must abide by the hierarchy Horus imposes. The prideful god has learned many harsh lessons about imposing his will upon other resurrected souls- the loss of the Ishmaelites dropped the ranks of his allies significantly and took him centuries to recover. Unofficially...well, most immortals from other factions as a rule try to avoid contact and tend to their own affairs and secrets...but the followers are the golden hawk are USEFUL in certain situations. Horus is as a rule the only being that offers an outstretched hand to all comers, which can be a useful thing when disputes between Andean immortals and Daoist mummies arise...or even when the deeply rooted aggression of millennia between old enemies can only be resolved through a outside third party. At the very top of his hierarchy are the Viziers, a trusted Mummy that attends to the affairs of an entire continent. Below these figures are the viceroys of Horus, beings that coordinate the affairs of importance in a given region called a waret (an area usually the size of a city but potentially far larger depending on the scope of the immortals interests and concerns in the region). Below each viceroy a Overseer typically attends to the affairs of specific cults or other important groups within the waret. Finally, each overseet is nominally in charge of a group of Assessors to attend to smaller matters or act as go betweens amongst the cults. Because immortals are so rare, a given city may have only one or two groups of general Assessors that move between Overseers and attened to undying affairs. The title of Overseer at times means little more than a designated ambassador, especially among non-egyptian mummies. They can call upon the aid of a group of Assessors as a courtesy and in turn agree to marginally act as a guide or host to traveling immortals in a strange land. Most other immortals, egyptians included, look with some level of disdain on the system as little more than a contrivance. But given the lack of a more formal system, and the general flexibility of the old Osirian League system they tend to make use of it, sometimes out of respect but more off simply for the sake of keeping the peace and because it helps keep each faction within certain defined boundaries of one another. There is some wisdom in aiding the various agendas of the immortals in their own battles, after all, and a hungry Child of Apophis or twisted servant of the Malfeans is just as happy to devour a South American mummy as it is child of the Nile.<br /> <br /> That's about it. What are your thoughts? Anything else you'd adjust to fit with the themes of the 20th anniversary and uncouple the setting from plot specific scenarios?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri","name":"nothri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"485"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-03T16:14:58-07:00","id":1482985,"title":"","content":"Check out Pillars of Ma\u00e1t on Storytellers Vault as its kind of a bootleg Mummy 20th.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/479-damian-may","name":"Damian May","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar479_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-04T13:50:46-07:00","id":1483067,"title":"","content":"There isn\u2019t a real world tradition of Taoist Mummies right? That concept in the game was sort of a rif on that being the true nature of alchemical immortality? Not criticizing just making sure I have my context right.<br /> <br /> (there is of course the self-mummified Buddhist Sokunshinbutsu, but that seems a different thing entirely)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-04T21:47:43-07:00","id":1483105,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1482311-mummy-20th-anniversary-ideas?p=1483067#post1483067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There isn\u2019t a real world tradition of Taoist Mummies right? That concept in the game was sort of a rif on that being the true nature of alchemical immortality? Not criticizing just making sure I have my context right.<br /> <br /> (there is of course the self-mummified Buddhist Sokunshinbutsu, but that seems a different thing entirely)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The idea probably comes from a combination of certain Taoist traditions (I say 'certain' traditions because Taoism has many schools of thought and the traditions have evolved in many ways over the course of 2200+ years) that one means of achieving immortality through a perfect balance of yin and yang (again, according to some traditions) can be induced by drinking an Elixir and certain interesting historical realities. Trying to create that Elixir ironically ended the lives of many, many chinese emperors and (presumably) nobles and scholars since one of the main ingredients with a lethal dose of Mercury. But Mercury has also been found in high concentrations in the bodies of various chinese emperors, wives, and nobles. A famous example is Xin Zhui, one of the best preserved mummies in the world. Daoism didn't view preservation as a necessity like the Egyptians did (although they do have interesting parallel ideas about the body needing grave goods and offerings from descendants to be happy in the afterlife). But an 'incorruptible' corpse that did not decay was a sign of enlightenment and that one had gotten closer if not achieved full immortality. So preservation and things that would cause preservation were of some interest to Taoists and other chinese funerary practices (as was often the case, Taoism had a habit of mixing their traditions with other local customs and other religions like Buddhism over the millennia). There are also examples of Song and Ming era mummies in China and later Korea that used similar funerary practices of sealing coffins and packing the corpse in tight with funeral garb that led to preservation, but we don't know if mummification was the end game or that these practices were related to Taoism as such.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri","name":"nothri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"485"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-05T08:16:06-07:00","id":1483128,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1482311-mummy-20th-anniversary-ideas?p=1483067#post1483067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There isn\u2019t a real world tradition of Taoist Mummies right? That concept in the game was sort of a rif on that being the true nature of alchemical immortality? Not criticizing just making sure I have my context right.<br /> <br /> (there is of course the self-mummified Buddhist Sokunshinbutsu, but that seems a different thing entirely)</div> </div> </div> </div>What <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2244\" data-vbnamecard=\"2244\">nothri</a> said, plus I think the game based it on the Sokunshinbutsu (know about them, not going to really verify the name though, so I'm simply copying yours).<br /> <br /> But the original two editions (I'm not sure about actual MtR text, saw it few times) did recognize mummies as a looser concept than just Egyptian-like or Egyptian-related corpses. The Spell of Life wasn't used just in Egypt and with time had variations being used all around the world. Even cultures without actual mummification traditions had some Mummies.<br /> <br /> It basically isn't saying that Mummies are a product of mummification practices, instead Mages got to bring the Spell to different places at different times, and it has its own history.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-06T21:58:53-07:00","id":1483280,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nothri</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1482311-mummy-20th-anniversary-ideas?p=1483105#post1483105\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> ......<br /> <br /> Daoism didn't view preservation as a necessity like the Egyptians did (although they do have interesting parallel ideas about the body needing grave goods and offerings from descendants to be happy in the afterlife). But an 'incorruptible' corpse that did not decay was a sign of enlightenment and that one had gotten closer if not achieved full immortality. So preservation and things that would cause preservation were of some interest to Taoists and other chinese funerary practices (as was often the case, Taoism had a habit of mixing their traditions with other local customs and other religions like Buddhism over the millennia). There are also examples of Song and Ming era mummies in China and later <b>Korea</b> that used similar funerary practices of sealing coffins and packing the corpse in tight with funeral garb that led to preservation, but we don't know if mummification was the end game or that these practices were related to Taoism as such.</div> </div> </div> </div>No Daoist beliefs related there - at least, none that <i>I'm</i> aware of. We were more likely to perceive the mysterious <i>absence</i> or inexplicable disappearance of the corpse as a sign of immortal ascension, rather than perfectly preserved corpses. At least one real-life historical figure is attributed to having done this, and he's currently a spiritual symbol of sorts for what little of seriously practicing Korean Daoists that remain.<br /> <br /> On topic: personally the Sokushinbutsu feels more apt as a &quot;Asian Undying&quot; splat, even though they're limited to Japan. In-universe, you can say that the Undying couldn't take hold in China and adjacent lands because of Wan Kuei dominance, and the Undying had to flee further and further East.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-07T05:10:49-07:00","id":1483301,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1482311-mummy-20th-anniversary-ideas?p=1483280#post1483280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On topic: personally the Sokushinbutsu feels more apt as a &quot;Asian Undying&quot; splat, even though they're limited to Japan. In-universe, you can say that the Undying couldn't take hold in China and adjacent lands because of Wan Kuei dominance, and the Undying had to flee further and further East.</div> </div> </div> </div>There are similar practices in india, tibet and east asia, but I can't find local terms for those. Sokushinbutsu might just be the best fit because no one else gave it a name other than &quot;buddhism&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-07T15:38:58-07:00","id":1483341,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1482311-mummy-20th-anniversary-ideas?p=1483301#post1483301\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There are similar practices in india, tibet and east asia, but I can't find local terms for those. Sokushinbutsu might just be the best fit because no one else gave it a name other than &quot;buddhism&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>They usually just call them <i>self-mummified</i> or <i>enlightened</i> in the local language AFAIK, and even then it isn't certain that those mummies really self-mummified instead of being mummified after death. Either way, they're rare, more a <i>thing that legend says to happen</i> among local Buddhists than a <i>tradition</i>. It only really took off as a more common practice for advanced masters in <b>Shugend\u014d</b>, and it is believed that hundreds at least tried it, but are still buried somewhere in Japan.<br /> <br /> Similar practices of ritual fasting meant to end one's life and shrinking of the body by dehydration and starvation do exist, like Sallekhana and Prayopavesa, but they're not meant to produce a preserved corpse. They're considered a very special way of ending one's life in pursuit of enlightenment or purification of Karma, never to be taken lightly, but the dead isn't necessarily considered already enlightened just because of that and the corpse is dealt with as normal, it is not a mummy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1482311-mummy-20th-anniversary-ideas?p=1483280#post1483280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No Daoist beliefs related there - at least, none that <i>I'm</i> aware of. We were more likely to perceive the mysterious <i>absence</i> or inexplicable disappearance of the corpse as a sign of immortal ascension, rather than perfectly preserved corpses. At least one real-life historical figure is attributed to having done this, and he's currently a spiritual symbol of sorts for what little of seriously practicing Korean Daoists that remain.<br /> <br /> On topic: personally the Sokushinbutsu feels more apt as a &quot;Asian Undying&quot; splat, even though they're limited to Japan. In-universe, you can say that the Undying couldn't take hold in China and adjacent lands because of Wan Kuei dominance, and the Undying had to flee further and further East.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's called <i><b>Rainbow Body</b></i>, at least by Buddhists, when enlightenment leads to the transformation of the body in &quot;light&quot;, either in death or, more rarely, in life. It is a far more common theme than self-mummification.<br /> <br /> For the Sokushinbutsu, I think the current 2nd edition lore works fine. Some few Buddhist Mages learned the Spell of Life and this was their own version of the ritual. A few have happened here and there through Asia as they did through Europe too, maybe even some of the few Rainbow Body happenstances may have been Mages becoming Mummies and magically leaving. The Shugend\u014d practitioners may have either perfected their version of the spell, or failed at it and produced common mummies, or even just been inspired by the Mages and started their own religious thing (as they did IRL).<br /> <br /> One thing to keep on mind is that <u><b>Mummies</b></u> aren't <i>mummies</i>. While the splat is inspired by <i>mummies</i> and meant to let you play the trope of a <i>mummy</i> raising from the dead, it never really implied that embalming practices have a greater relation to it other than being a part of the ritual, quite the opposite, they explicitly still predate it and exist as their own thing. In all editions most lower-case <i>mummies </i>aren't anything more than just normal corpses, and upper-case <b><u>Mummies</u></b> usually aren't found embalmed, they're wandering around and doing their stuff and when dead the corpse is completely normal until the Ba comes back to inhabit it. Most <b><u>Mummies</u></b> will try to prepare for the preservation of the corpse because that makes coming back easier, but isn't a requirement.<br /> <br /> And once stripped of religious connotation, embalming of some sort isn't something unheard of in most places through most of history, just being rare as few people saw any reason to do it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":".  typo 06-07-2022, 06:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-07T16:31:14-07:00","id":1483346,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1482311-mummy-20th-anniversary-ideas?p=1483341#post1483341\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's called <i><b>Rainbow Body</b></i>, at least by Buddhists, when enlightenment leads to the transformation of the body in &amp;quot;light&amp;quot;, either in death or, more rarely, in life. It is a far more common theme than self-mummification.<br /> <br /> For the Sokushinbutsu, I think the current 2nd edition lore works fine. Some few Buddhist Mages learned the Spell of Life and this was their own version of the ritual. A few have happened here and there through Asia as they did through Europe too, maybe even some of the few Rainbow Body happenstances may have been Mages becoming Mummies and magically leaving. The Shugend\u014d practitioners may have either perfected their version of the spell, or failed at it and produced common mummies, or even just been inspired by the Mages and started their own religious thing (as they did IRL).<br /> <br /> One thing to keep on mind is that <u><b>Mummies</b></u> aren't <i>mummies</i>. While the splat is inspired by <i>mummies</i> and meant to let you play the trope of a <i>mummy</i> raising from the dead, it never really implied that embalming practices have a greater relation to it other than being a part of the ritual, quite the opposite, they explicitly still predate it and exist as their own thing. In all editions most lower-case <i>mummies </i>aren't anything more than just normal corpses, and upper-case <b><u>Mummies</u></b> usually aren't found embalmed, they're wandering around and doing their stuff and when dead the corpse is completely normal until the Ba comes back to inhabit it. Most <b><u>Mummies</u></b> will try to prepare for the preservation of the corpse because that makes coming back easier, but isn't a requirement.<br /> <br /> And once stripped of religious connotation, embalming of some sort isn't something unheard of in most places through most of history, just being rare as few people saw any reason to do it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mmm, never heard of the word Rainbow Body before. Must be <i>not</i> from Mahayana, I guess.<br /> <br /> Still, I can\u2019t help but think the Undying are better fit as Buddhist-derived, as opposed to Daoist-derived, for some reason\u2026 Maybe my head associates the latter too much with the Wan Kuei. Plus, I feel a Buddhist base would give them a more visible connection to European lands, than a Daoist base would do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-08T10:24:37-07:00","id":1483385,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1482311-mummy-20th-anniversary-ideas?p=1483346#post1483346\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mmm, never heard of the word Rainbow Body before. Must be <i>not</i> from Mahayana, I guess.<br /> <br /> Still, I can\u2019t help but think the Undying are better fit as Buddhist-derived, as opposed to Daoist-derived, for some reason\u2026 Maybe my head associates the latter too much with the Wan Kuei. Plus, I feel a Buddhist base would give them a more visible connection to European lands, than a Daoist base would do.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed it is more of a Vajrayana thing, specially Indo-Tibetan Dzogchen.<br /> <br /> On the matter of the Undying vs the Wan-Kuei, I get your impression, and it must be even stronger for you than for me as both are closer to you than to me in daily life, but in the end the two splats distinguish themselves more on the basis of how you get there than on your beliefs. If there is a Mage with access to the Spell of Life, they can tweak the ritual in several ways to fit their Paradigm and turn their chosen target into a <b><u>Mummy</u></b>. You become a <b><u>Wan-Kuei</u></b> when you go to a Hell and crawl back to Earth. None need a specific set of beliefs themselves to go through either process (the Chosen for the Spell of Life may be just the highest bidder, the Mage uses it however they want).<br /> <br /> What would really be distinct about the Sokushinbutsu is that it would mean a Mage doing the Spell of Life on themselves, which would be really a very special scenario as they're giving up the Awakened Avatar and its reincarnation. Not impossible at all, but it puts a lot more twists and specifics in defining this character, and probably wouldn't be about enlightenment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-09T09:05:21-07:00","id":1483469,"title":"","content":"Rainbow Body and Dzogchen could provide a lot of cool ideas for Indo-Tibetan/trans-Himalayan mummies, I think. And there's loads of cool stuff from the Tibetan Book of the Dead that could be used to draw from (see also Bardo, the RL version and not the Discipline).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}